---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleSchema from "../../components/ArticleSchema.astro";
import FaqSection from "../../components/FaqSection.astro";
import { site, description } from "../../content";
const path = "/guides/consent-compliance";
const title = "Build a traceable Shopify email permission system";
const desc = "Map capture, permission history and opt-outs before enabling promotional journeys.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is a purchase enough for marketing permission?",
    "answer": "No universal answer applies. Review the applicable jurisdiction and capture history; the UK products-and-services soft opt-in has specific conditions beyond purchase."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is double opt-in always mandatory?",
    "answer": "No blanket rule is established here. It can strengthen confirmation evidence; choose it with the applicable rules and capture risks in mind."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I restore an unsubscribed profile during migration?",
    "answer": "Do not infer renewed permission from an import or purchase. Preserve withdrawal history and resolve conflicts before promotional enrollment."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does email permission cover SMS?",
    "answer": "Do not treat email permission or a delivery phone number as SMS marketing permission. Review SMS requirements separately before enabling that channel."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Build a traceable Shopify email permission system</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Map capture, permission history and opt-outs before enabling promotional journeys.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Separate the legal decision from the integration setting</h2><p>A subscribed flag is an input, not a legal conclusion. Record the countries served, recipient type, channel, intended message and basis approved for that audience. Keep unresolved imported records out of promotional enrollment until their history is reviewed. This guide covers selected US and UK email rules; EU member-state, Canadian, Australian and SMS requirements need their own jurisdiction-specific review. No platform makes a store compliant by default.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>US commercial email: audit the whole template</h2><p>The FTC CAN-SPAM guide calls for accurate sender information, truthful subjects, clear advertising identification, a valid postal address and an easy opt-out. The mechanism must work for at least 30 days after sending, and requests must be honored within 10 business days. Treat that as a legal outer limit, not a reason to delay suppression. Adopt prompt processing and test queued messages as well as future enrollment. This US rule is not a substitute for other applicable laws or mailbox-provider policies.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>UK consumers: test the exception, do not assume it</h2><p>ICO guidance distinguishes permission to send electronic marketing under PECR from the UK GDPR basis for processing personal data. For unsolicited marketing to individual subscribers, consent or an applicable soft opt-in is needed. The products-and-services soft opt-in concerns your own similar offerings to customers whose details you collected during a sale or negotiations, with an opt-out at collection and in each message. A purchase alone is insufficient. Legitimate interests under data protection law does not bypass PECR. Double opt-in can provide evidence of confirmation; it is not a blanket statutory requirement for every subscriber.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Build a capture register</h2><p>For checkout, newsletter footer, popup, waitlist and event signup, retain form identifier, displayed wording/version, timestamp, channel, source and resulting state. Decide which records are necessary and how long to retain them; collecting extra personal data is not proof of better consent. A product availability alert should not silently enroll someone in unrelated promotions. Keep giveaway administration separate from marketing choice. A phone supplied for delivery does not demonstrate permission for promotional SMS.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Proposed sync acceptance test</h2><p>Use controlled test identities, not real customer sends. Submit each capture path with and without marketing permission; compare the Shopify record, integration payload and destination profile. Repeat opt-out from the email footer and preference center, then inspect all senders and already queued journeys. Test duplicate identities, guest checkout and delayed/replayed events. The expected result is that an old subscribe event cannot silently overwrite a later withdrawal. Save timestamps and actual payload fields with the integration version. No such test is reported as executed here.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Migration and re-permission</h2><p>Export suppression history before subscriber rows. Preserve source status, timestamp and provenance; quarantine conflicting or missing records rather than inferring permission from orders or opens. Requesting consent by email can itself be marketing, so a re-permission campaign cannot automatically repair a list that had no basis for contact. Have the applicable rules reviewed before sending. Retain the minimum suppression information needed to prevent accidental reimport while handling access and erasure requests through the documented privacy process.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Keep service ownership explicit</h2><p>The FTC treats transactional/relationship categories narrowly and evaluates mixed messages by primary purpose. A purchase relationship does not make every follow-up transactional. Review subject, placement and prominence of promotional copy before adding an offer to a shipping notice. Maintain a template register with classification, owner and revision date. Marketing opt-outs and essential service delivery need distinct controls; do not use that distinction to route promotions through a service sender.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Ask for observable controls in the demo</h2><p>If considering Sequenzy or another sender, request a demonstration using the same capture, withdrawal, replay and export cases. Ask who owns suppression across integrations, whether queued sends recheck eligibility, and how consent evidence is exported. Record unsupported cases as implementation gaps. A screenshot of a consent checkbox or an assurance from sales does not prove end-to-end behavior.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Primary references and related decisions</h2><p>Primary guidance checked 24 November 2026; scope is limited to the points attributed above. Store configuration and jurisdiction-specific review remain necessary.</p><ul><li><a class="link" href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business">FTC CAN-SPAM guide</a></li><li><a class="link" href="https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/direct-marketing-and-privacy-and-electronic-communications/guide-to-pecr/electronic-and-telephone-marketing/electronic-mail-marketing/">ICO electronic mail marketing</a></li></ul><p><a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">Deliverability</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">Discount discipline</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/flow-collision-prevention">Flow collision prevention</a></p></div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
